From 0916386357b03da48a95c68f0189abb1b2dfd02b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: c4ffein <c4ffein.work@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 21:18:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 236/248] Bugfix: recompute node hash position after table
 resize in `vmeshSeparateFm()` [report PyScotch]

When the element hash table is full while the initial gains of the
frontier nodes are being set, vmeshSeparateFmResize() re-hashes all
tables and doubles the hash mask, so that the node being processed,
which had just been inserted, moves to another slot. The local index
hnodnum was not recomputed, whereas it is used right after to set the
part and the element counter of that node. This corrupted another slot
(or an empty one), resulting in "internal error (4)" in debug mode or in
a crash.

The other two resize call sites already recompute all their indices.

Tested with an instrumented build in which the initial table size can
be reduced: m2 200x200 mesh, mord crashed (segmentation fault) before
the fix and gives a valid ordering after, also under ASan/UBSan with
SCOTCH_DEBUG_VMESH2/VMESH3. With the standard SCOTCH_DEBUG_VMESH2 build
(initial size divided by 8), mord on a 400x400 mesh failed with
"vmeshSeparateFm: internal error (4)" before the fix and succeeds after.
test_scotch_mesh_order passes.

Debug and normal builds can give different (valid, comparable quality)
orderings because forcing resizes changes the gain table link order.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/libscotch/vmesh_separate_fm.c | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/libscotch/vmesh_separate_fm.c b/src/libscotch/vmesh_separate_fm.c
index d9b900c..519b398 100644
--- a/src/libscotch/vmesh_separate_fm.c
+++ b/src/libscotch/vmesh_separate_fm.c
@@ -468,6 +468,8 @@ const VmeshSeparateFmParam * restrict const paraptr) /*+ Method parameters    +*
             hashsiz <<= 1;
             hashmsk   = (hashmsk << 1) | 1;
 
+            for (hnodnum = (vnodnum * VMESHSEPAFMHASHPRIME) & hashmsk; /* Re-compute position of current node, which has moved */
+                 hnodtab[hnodnum].vnodnum != vnodnum; hnodnum = (hnodnum + 1) & hashmsk) ;
             for (helmnum = (velmnum * VMESHSEPAFMHASHPRIME) & hashmsk; /* Re-compute position in table */
                  helmtab[helmnum].velmnum != ~0; helmnum = (helmnum + 1) & hashmsk) ;
           }
